<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">5.1 Section Exercises</heading>
    <heading level="1">Verbal</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Explain the advantage of writing a quadratic function in standard form.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>When written in that form, the vertex can be easily identified.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>How can the vertex of a parabola be used in solving real-world problems?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Explain why the condition of $$a \neq 0$$ is imposed in the definition of the quadratic function.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>If $$a=0 a=0$$ then the function becomes a linear function.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>What is another name for the standard form of a quadratic function?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="5" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>What two algebraic methods can be used to find the horizontal intercepts of a quadratic function?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>If possible, we can use factoring. Otherwise, we can use the quadratic formula.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
